# Exercise 03: Windows Service Startup Types and Configuration

**Project:** Windows System Administration Lab  
**Author:** kuronoi  
**Status:** Completed (Simulated Practical Exercise)

## 1. Objective
To understand Windows service startup types and how administrators configure services using Services Manager.

## 2. Scenario Description
An organisation needed to configure Windows services so that important services could start automatically while other services would only start when required.

The IT administrator needed to understand the different startup types and their purposes.

## 3. Tools Used
- Windows 11
- Services Manager (`services.msc`)
- Windows Service Properties

## 4. Procedure
1. Opened Services Manager.
2. Identified the available service startup types.
3. Examined the purpose of Automatic startup.
4. Examined Automatic (Delayed Start).
5. Reviewed the purpose of Manual startup.
6. Identified how Disabled prevents a service from starting normally.
7. Reviewed how to change startup types through the service Properties window.
8. Considered the importance of checking service dependencies before making configuration changes.

## 5. Results
The simulated exercise demonstrated the differences between Windows service startup types and how administrators configure them.

## 6. Lessons Learned
- Meaning of Automatic startup.
- Purpose of Automatic (Delayed Start).
- Difference between Manual and Disabled.
- How to access service Properties.
- How to configure startup types.
- Importance of understanding service dependencies.

## 7. Conclusion
This simulated exercise improved my understanding of Windows service configuration and how startup settings affect system functionality.

**Note:** This was a simulated training exercise. No actual Windows service configurations were changed.

